# Mutation plan for the twelve local-mode pairs of `bot_local.go`, `status_local.go` and
# `role_local.go`.
#
#   scripts/mutate-batch.sh scripts/mutations/local-families.plan
#
# The handlers themselves are the HTTP ones and are mutated by their own plans. What is new here,
# and all this file tests, is the **wiring**: which session the socket hands them, which segments
# reach them at all, and where a request that does not reach them is forwarded to.
#
# The forwarding target is the line worth reading twice. Forwarding a local request over the
# *port* instead of the socket reaches `APISessionRequired` rather than `APILocal`, so a malformed
# id comes back **401** where Go answers **404** — a difference no test that only compares bodies
# would notice, which is why `a_segment_outside_the_mux_charset_is_forwarded_over_the_socket`
# asserts the status too.
#
# `control-reorder-two-independent-registrations` moves one `.route` call past another with a
# different path. Registration order is not routing order in axum — matchit builds a tree — so a
# passing suite after the move is the control it is meant to be.
#
# The first version of both controls was broken rather than no-op, and each failed in its own way:
# renaming `let matched` without renaming `if !matched` does not compile, and a "reorder" written
# as a prepend **duplicated** a route, which axum rejects at startup with "Overlapping method
# route". Neither is a verdict; a control that does not build proves nothing about the harness.
# They are a contiguous rename of a binding *and its use*, and a true swap, now.
#
# # The valid run: **11 run, 9 caught, 2 controls survived, 0 harness faults** (2026-09-12)
#
# Every mutation is caught, which for a registration round means what it should: the wiring is
# what these tests are about, and there is no logic here of the kind that hides behind a fixture.
#
# # What the first (aborted) run found
#
# Three lines were removed because they are **equivalent mutants**, and saying so costs less than
# leaving them to be re-derived:
#
# * `local-the-role-name-charset-accepts-upper-case` and `…-accepts-a-hyphen`. Loosening the
#   middleware's `role_name` class lets the request through to `roles::get_role_by_name`, whose
#   own `[a-z0-9_]+` check then forwards it — over the **port**. That looks like the bug this
#   middleware exists to prevent, and it is not observable: measured against the stack's Go server,
#   `GET /api/v4/roles/name/System_User` and `GET /api/v4/bots/bad-id` are **404
#   `api.context.404.app_error`** on the HTTP router *and* on the local one, because neither mux
#   matches and `Handle404` runs before any session is required. Same status, same body, same
#   interpolated URL.
# * `local-a-bad-segment-is-forwarded-over-the-port` did not compile: the middleware takes
#   `Extension(go)`, not `State(state)`, so there is no state in scope to hand `forward_to_go`.
#
# The transport *is* observable, at the other forwarding site: `GET /api/v4/roles/names` is a
# **400** over the socket (Go's local mux matches `{role_id}` and `getRole("names")` refuses) and a
# **401** over the port (the same route behind `APISessionRequired`, with no credential on a socket
# request). `local-the-method-fallback-forwards-over-the-port` is that line, and
# `the_role_reads_match_over_the_socket` is what catches it.
#
# `local-the-status-write-uses-an-ordinary-session` also survived, and that one *was* a fixture
# gap: the only PUT the suite made carried a body Go rejects, so it never reached
# `session_has_permission_to_user`. The test now also sets a planted bot's status through each
# socket, which is a 200 only because the session is `Local`.
